// Case-level defaults for the 3D stationary droplet (sphere R = 1 mm in a
// 0.01^3 m^3 cube) -- the 3D twin of stationaryDroplet2D.parameter.
//
// WHY A 3D TWIN. Everything the curvature campaign has measured coupled is 2D,
// and two of its central objects are dimension-specific:
//   * the delivered kappa_f error is a single-valued function of the angle
//     between the CSF face normal and the interface normal, and on a Cartesian
//     2D mesh that angle sweeps 0-90 deg FOUR times around the circle -- the
//     cos(4 theta) pole bias. In 3D the same mechanism is a solid-angle
//     effect over three face orientations, not a 4-fold ring.
//   * the parallel-surface inverse needs the Gaussian curvature K in 3D
//     (K = 0 identically in 2D), so the delivery exercises a term the 2D runs
//     never touch. The static 3D sphere gate already scores it (h^1.95); this
//     case is what makes the COUPLED 3D behaviour measurable.
// Mean curvature is 2/R here, so the exact Laplace jump is sigma*2/R = 145.5 Pa
// against 72.7 Pa in 2D. createDropletMetricsFile.H already computes
// kappaExactDrop = (nGeometricD - 1)/R, so the metrics are dimension-aware
// without a change.
//
// RESOLUTIONS. Cost is N^3, so the 2D ladder does not carry over: N=32 is
// 32768 cells (cheap), N=64 is 262144, N=96 is 884736. The capillary step is
// unchanged in FORM -- dt_sigma ~ sqrt(rho h^3 / (2 pi sigma)) gives
// MAX_DELTA_T = CAPILLARY_DT_COEFF/N^1.5 in any dimension -- so the step and
// the step COUNT to a given END_TIME are the same as 2D at equal N; only the
// per-step cost grows.
values
{
    solver (leiaSemiLagrangianLevelSetTwoPhaseFoam);
    N_CELLS
    (
        32
        64
        96
    );
    END_TIME ( 0.1 );
    MAX_CO ( 0.1 );
    CAPILLARY_DT_COEFF ( 0.010861 );
    SL_RECONSTRUCTION ( uncachedQuadraticWeightedLeastSquares );
}
